Rem ==================================================================
Rem math.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: the derived math layer.
Rem
Rem The runtime supplies the primitives that need native precision or
Rem native state, and all thirty are documented one by one immediately
Rem below: + - * / MOD WRAP, ABS SGN NEGATE, MIN MAX, FLOOR CEIL ROUND
Rem FIX, ^ SQR EXP LOG LOG10, SIN COS TAN ATN ATN2 ASIN ACOS TANH, PI,
Rem and SEED. Everything after them is expressed on top of them, the way
Rem clock.shoddy is derived from the Ticks builtin — no runtime support
Rem of its own. Standalone: it includes nothing, so a program can pull in
Rem math alone.
Rem
Rem RND IS NOT HERE, THOUGH THIS FILE CALLS IT. RandRange, RandInt,
Rem Pick and Chance below are all Rnd underneath — but a builtin is
Rem documented in ONE machine, and Rnd's is random.shoddy, whose whole
Rem header is a claim on it ("built directly on the Rnd builtin") where
Rem this file's four uses are game-flavoured convenience. See
Rem random.shoddy for what Rnd answers and why it is impure.
Rem
Rem SEED IS HERE, and the split from Rnd is deliberate rather than
Rem tidy. random.shoddy is seedless BY CONSTRUCTION — its header says
Rem in as many words that there is no seed to create, store or thread
Rem through your program — so the one word that reseeds the generator
Rem would contradict the machine it otherwise belongs beside. It is
Rem documented here instead, with the arithmetic primitives it sits
Rem among in the runtime, and it reseeds random.shoddy's words just as
Rem it reseeds this file's.
Rem
Rem Naming: no Def here may shadow a builtin (a Def silently outranks
Rem one), which is why the base-10 log stays the LOG10 builtin and this
Rem file adds only Log2/LogBase.
Rem ==================================================================

Rem ---- the builtins this machine is built on -------------------------
Rem
Rem NOT Defs. The engine dispatches these, and a Def whose name is a
Rem builtin is refused; they are written here because math.shoddy is
Rem where a reader goes looking for them. The flat reference is section
Rem 10 of docs/spec.html, and the stack effects below are the ones the
Rem compiler's own Effects.cs table declares.
Rem
Rem NUMBER IS THE ONLY NUMERIC TYPE — a double. There is no integer
Rem type, so "whole number" everywhere below means a double with no
Rem fractional part, exact to 2^53. Angles are RADIANS at every
Rem boundary; Rad and Deg below convert. Every one of these is pure
Rem except SEED.

Rem ---- arithmetic ----

Rem a + b   a - b   a * b   a / b                     ( a b -- c )
Rem     The four operators. + also joins two STRINGs, which is why the
Rem     reckoner's dictionary needs no separate word for &.

Rem a Mod b As Number                                 ( a b -- r )
Rem     The remainder, taking its sign from a — the truncated form, as
Rem     in BASIC and C. Wrap is the one that does not.

Rem Wrap(a As Number, b As Number) As Number          ( a b -- r )
Rem     FLOORED modulo: the answer takes its sign from b, so
Rem     Wrap(-1, 360) is 359 where -1 Mod 360 is -1. This is the one for
Rem     angles, ring buffers and anything that comes round again.

Rem a ^ b As Number                                   ( a b -- c )
Rem     a raised to the power b. Tightest-binding operator there is.

Rem Negate(x As Number) As Number                     ( x -- -x )
Rem     The number with its sign turned round.

Rem Abs(x As Number) As Number                        ( x -- y )
Rem     Its magnitude, sign discarded.

Rem Sgn(x As Number) As Number                        ( x -- s )
Rem     -1, 0 or 1, according to the sign.

Rem Min(a As Number, b As Number) As Number           ( a b -- c )
Rem Max(a As Number, b As Number) As Number           ( a b -- c )
Rem     The smaller and the larger of the two. Clamp below is both at
Rem     once, and is what a bounded value actually wants.

Rem Sqr(x As Number) As Number                        ( x -- y )
Rem     The square root. Named for BASIC's SQR and not for squaring —
Rem     squaring is x ^ 2, or x * x.

Rem ---- rounding, and there are four of them ----
Rem
Rem THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS AND IS EASY TO GET WRONG. Floor and Ceil go
Rem one way each regardless of sign; Fix goes towards zero, so it and
Rem Floor differ on every negative number; Round goes to the nearest.

Rem Floor(x As Number) As Number                      ( x -- n )
Rem     Down, towards minus infinity. Floor(-2.5) is -3.

Rem Ceil(x As Number) As Number                       ( x -- n )
Rem     Up, towards plus infinity. Ceil(-2.5) is -2.

Rem Round(x As Number) As Number                      ( x -- n )
Rem     To the nearest whole number. RoundTo below rounds to a given
Rem     number of places, and ToFixed in str.shoddy formats.

Rem Fix(x As Number) As Number                        ( x -- n )
Rem     Towards zero — the fractional part simply dropped. Fix(-2.5) is
Rem     -2, where Floor(-2.5) is -3.

Rem ---- exponential and logarithmic ----

Rem Exp(x As Number) As Number                        ( x -- y )
Rem     e raised to the power x.

Rem Log(x As Number) As Number                        ( x -- y )
Rem     The NATURAL logarithm, base e. The name is BASIC's, and it is
Rem     the one people misread: base ten is Log10.

Rem Log10(x As Number) As Number                      ( x -- y )
Rem     The base-ten logarithm. Log2 and LogBase below are derived from
Rem     these two, since the runtime supplies no other base.

Rem ---- trigonometry, in radians ----

Rem Sin(x As Number) As Number                        ( rad -- y )
Rem Cos(x As Number) As Number                        ( rad -- y )
Rem Tan(x As Number) As Number                        ( rad -- y )
Rem     The three, taking radians. Rad below converts from degrees.

Rem Atn(x As Number) As Number                        ( x -- rad )
Rem     The arctangent, in radians. Atn2 is the one that knows which
Rem     quadrant the point is in.

Rem Atn2(y As Number, x As Number) As Number          ( y x -- rad )
Rem     The angle of the point (x, y), in radians, in the right
Rem     quadrant. Note the argument order: y first, as in C's atan2.

Rem Asin(x As Number) As Number                       ( x -- rad )
Rem Acos(x As Number) As Number                       ( x -- rad )
Rem     The inverse sine and cosine, in radians. Outside -1 .. 1 there
Rem     is no real answer.

Rem Tanh(x As Number) As Number                       ( x -- y )
Rem     The hyperbolic tangent. Saturates towards 1 and -1, so it never
Rem     overflows — which is why neural.shoddy uses it as its activation
Rem     function. The other eleven hyperbolics are eng.shoddy's.

Rem Pi() As Number                                    ( -- pi )
Rem     The constant. Tau below is two of them, and E is eng's and
Rem     this file's rather than the runtime's — Exp(1) is where it comes
Rem     from.

Rem ---- the one impure word here ----

Rem Seed(n As Number)                                 ( n -- )
Rem     Reseed the engine's random generator, so that Rnd — and
Rem     everything built on it, including random.shoddy's Random,
Rem     RandomRange, RandomInt, Shuffle and Sample, and this file's own
Rem     RandRange, RandInt, Pick and Chance — answers reproducibly from
Rem     here on. The only word in this file with an effect, and the
Rem     reason the game-flavoured randomness below is not pure.

Rem ---- this machine's own words --------------------------------------

Rem ---- constants -----------------------------------------------------

Def E() As Number
    Exp(1)

Def Tau() As Number
    2 * Pi()

Rem ---- angle conversion ----------------------------------------------
Rem Trig builtins take radians; these bridge to degrees (turtles, UIs).

Def Rad(deg As Number) As Number
    deg * Pi() / 180

Def Deg(rad As Number) As Number
    rad * 180 / Pi()

Rem Direction of a vector as an angle, radians in (-Pi, Pi]. Atn2's
Rem argument order is (y, x); Angle takes (dx, dy) to read naturally.
Def Angle(dx As Number, dy As Number) As Number
    Atn2(dy, dx)

Rem ---- parts and rounding --------------------------------------------

Rem Fractional part, sign of x (Fix truncates toward zero, so -3.25 -> -0.25).
Def Frac(x As Number) As Number
    x - Fix(x)

Rem Round to n decimal places.
Def RoundTo(x As Number, n As Number) As Number
    Let s = 10 ^ n
    Round(x * s) / s

Rem ---- range shaping -------------------------------------------------

Def Clamp(x As Number, lo As Number, hi As Number) As Number
    Max(lo, Min(hi, x))

Def Lerp(a As Number, b As Number, t As Number) As Number
    a + (b - a) * t

Rem Where x sits in [a, b] as a fraction — the inverse of Lerp.
Def InvLerp(a As Number, b As Number, x As Number) As Number
    (x - a) / (b - a)

Rem Map x from the input range onto the output range, linearly.
Def Remap(x As Number, inLo As Number, inHi As Number, outLo As Number, outHi As Number) As Number
    Lerp(outLo, outHi, InvLerp(inLo, inHi, x))

Rem Smooth Hermite interpolation, clamped to [0, 1] at the edges.
Def Smoothstep(edge0 As Number, edge1 As Number, x As Number) As Number
    Let t = Clamp(InvLerp(edge0, edge1, x), 0, 1)
    t * t * (3 - 2 * t)

Rem ---- geometry ------------------------------------------------------

Def Hypot(x As Number, y As Number) As Number
    Sqr(x * x + y * y)

Def Dist(x0 As Number, y0 As Number, x1 As Number, y1 As Number) As Number
    Hypot(x1 - x0, y1 - y0)

Rem ---- logarithms ----------------------------------------------------
Rem Natural (LOG) and base-10 (LOG10) are builtins; other bases derive.

Def Log2(x As Number) As Number
    Log(x) / Log(2)

Def LogBase(base As Number, x As Number) As Number
    Log(x) / Log(base)

Rem ---- random --------------------------------------------------------
Rem All on top of RND (float in [0, 1)); SEED makes any of them
Rem reproducible.

Rem Float in [lo, hi).
Def RandRange(lo As Number, hi As Number) As Number
    lo + Rnd() * (hi - lo)

Rem Integer in [lo, hi], both ends inclusive.
Def RandInt(lo As Number, hi As Number) As Number
    Floor(Rnd() * (hi - lo + 1)) + lo

Rem A uniformly-chosen element of a non-empty list or array.
Def Pick(xs As List Of t) As t
    Nth(xs, Floor(Rnd() * Length(xs)) + 1)

Rem True with probability p (p <= 0 never, p >= 1 always).
Def Chance(p As Number) As Boolean
    Rnd() < p
